---
import DocsPage from "../../layouts/DocsPage.astro"

export const prerender = true
---

<DocsPage id="mcp-and-plugins">

          <p>
            MCP servers and repository connections give an agent structured access to approved tools. Vector's catalog
            lists 133 connectors — GitHub, Linear, Sentry, Figma, Playwright, Supabase, Stripe, Notion and more — plus
            any MCP server you add yourself. Connectors marked Soon are listed but cannot be connected yet.
          </p>

          <h2 id="connections">Connect a server</h2>
          <p>
            Open <strong>Plugins</strong> or <strong>Connections</strong> from the workspace rail. Add only services you
            trust, confirm the permissions they request, and verify the connection before depending on it in a task.
          </p>
          <p class="note">
            A listed integration is not automatically connected. It becomes available only after its
            setup succeeds and Vector reports it as active.
          </p>
          <ul class="feature-list">
            <li><strong>Plugin runtimes that install themselves</strong><span>A catalog plugin that needs uvx no longer becomes a terminal errand. Vector resolves the runner, installs it through Astral's own installer when it is missing, and rewrites the command to an absolute path so it spawns without a login shell.</span></li>
            <li><strong>Servers that stop answering</strong><span>MCP servers that stop answering are recovered instead of wedging the app.</span></li>
          </ul>

          <h2 id="cli-mcp">Custom MCP servers from the terminal</h2>
          <p>
            <code>vector mcp add</code> registers a server: <code>--url</code> for a remote one with <code>--header KEY=VALUE</code>, or a command with <code>--env KEY=VALUE</code> for a local one. <code>vector mcp list</code> shows every configured server and whether it is reachable.
          </p>
          <p class="doc-cmd"><code>vector mcp add linear --url https://mcp.linear.app/sse
vector mcp auth linear
vector mcp list</code><button class="doc-cmd-copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy this command">Copy</button></p>
          <p>
            For OAuth-protected servers, <code>vector mcp auth</code> runs the flow, <code>vector mcp auth list</code> shows which servers are OAuth-capable and their status, <code>vector mcp logout</code> drops stored credentials, and <code>vector mcp debug &lt;name&gt;</code> diagnoses a failing connection.
          </p>

          <h2 id="mcp-config">MCP servers in vector.json</h2>
          <p>
            Servers live under the <code>mcp</code> key of <code>vector.json</code>, where <code>vector mcp add</code>
            writes them and where you can write them yourself (see <a href="/docs/configuration">Configuration
            files</a>). Servers you add in the desktop app are kept per repository in Vector's own state folder, not
            in <code>vector.json</code>. Each entry's key is the server's name, and <code>type</code> says how Vector reaches it:
            <code>remote</code> for a server at an address, <code>local</code> for a program Vector starts on this
            computer.
          </p>
          <pre><code>{`{
  "mcp": {
    "issues": {
      "type": "remote",
      "url": "https://mcp.example.com/mcp",
      "headers": { "Authorization": "Bearer {env:ISSUES_TOKEN}" }
    },
    "docs-search": {
      "type": "local",
      "command": ["node", "./tools/docs-mcp.js"],
      "environment": { "DOCS_INDEX": "./build/index" },
      "timeout": 10000
    }
  }
}`}</code></pre>
          <dl class="doc-rows">
            <div><dt>url (remote)</dt><dd>The server's address. Vector connects with Streamable HTTP and falls back to SSE. In your own settings it must be <code>https://</code>, or <code>http://</code> to this computer or your own network; in a repository's settings, <code>https://</code> or <code>http://</code> to this computer only. An address with a username or password inside it is refused.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>headers (remote)</dt><dd>Headers sent with every request to the server.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>oauth (remote)</dt><dd>Vector detects OAuth by itself: a server that asks for a sign-in shows as needing authentication, and <code>vector mcp auth &lt;name&gt;</code> runs the flow. Without <code>clientId</code>, Vector registers itself with the server; a server that does not allow that needs <code>oauth.clientId</code>, and <code>clientSecret</code> if it requires one. <code>scope</code> sets the scopes asked for, and the sign-in returns to <code>http://127.0.0.1:19876/mcp/oauth/callback</code> unless you set <code>redirectUri</code>, or <code>callbackPort</code> for another port. <code>"oauth": false</code> turns detection off, for a server you authenticate with headers.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>command (local)</dt><dd>The program and its arguments, as a list.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>environment (local)</dt><dd>Variables added to the environment Vector passes on to the program.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>cwd (local)</dt><dd>The program's working folder, relative to the folder you work in (for a repository's own server, the repository's root). Until you trust the repository, a server from your own settings starts in an empty folder of Vector's instead, so the repository's files cannot steer it.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>timeout</dt><dd>How long Vector waits, in milliseconds, for the server to connect and list its tools (30 seconds by default), and for each request to it, tool calls included (by default <code>experimental.mcp_timeout</code>, or 60 seconds when that is not set). A tool call that reports progress starts its wait again. After two requests in a row time out, Vector disconnects the server and marks it failed until you connect it again.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>enabled</dt><dd><code>false</code> keeps the server in your settings without starting it. An entry with only <code>"enabled": false</code> turns off a server that another settings file defines.</dd></div>
          </dl>
          <p>
            Values in <code>headers</code>, <code>command</code> and <code>environment</code> can name a secret
            instead of holding it. <code>{"{env:NAME}"}</code> is the environment variable <code>NAME</code>, filled in
            when Vector loads the settings file. <code>{"{vault:NAME}"}</code> is a credential Vector keeps for that
            server in its own credential store, outside every settings file: connecting a catalog plugin that asks for a
            token in the desktop app's Plugins stores the token that way. A server whose <code>{"{vault:}"}</code>
            credential is missing does not start, and its error names the credential. When you add a remote server in
            the desktop app, a header that looks like a credential (<code>Authorization</code>, <code>Cookie</code>,
            <code>Proxy-Authorization</code>, or a name containing <code>api-key</code>, <code>token</code>,
            <code>secret</code> or <code>password</code>) must be such a reference, such as
            <code>{"Bearer {env:MCP_TOKEN}"}</code>, so the key itself is never written to a settings file.
          </p>
          <p>
            A server's tools reach the agent named <code>&lt;server&gt;_&lt;tool&gt;</code>, with any character other
            than letters, digits, <code>-</code> and <code>_</code> turned into <code>_</code>. That is the name a
            <a href="/docs/permissions">permission rule</a> matches, so <code>{`"issues_*": "ask"`}</code> asks before
            any of the <code>issues</code> server's tools runs. A server's prompts become
            <a href="/docs/skills-and-commands#commands">commands</a> named <code>&lt;server&gt;:&lt;prompt&gt;</code>.
            A repository's servers start only once you trust it.
          </p>

          <h2 id="cli-plugins">Plugins</h2>
          <p>
            <code>vector plugin &lt;module&gt;</code> installs an npm module as a plugin and writes it into your config; <code>--global</code> installs into the global config rather than the repository's, and <code>--force</code> replaces a version already there. A plugin can register extra tools, which the registry merges alongside the built-in ones.
          </p>
          <p class="doc-cmd"><code>vector plugin @acme/vector-plugin -g</code><button class="doc-cmd-copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy this command">Copy</button></p>
          <p>
            For new settings, use Vector names: <code>vector.json</code>,
            <code>vector.jsonc</code>, <code>.vector/</code>, and <code>VECTOR_*</code> variables, which 1.99.99 is the first release to read. It recognizes earlier config files and repository folders by their contents,
            imports them into Vector paths once, and preserves originals. Ambiguous or conflicting security
            settings require repair before startup. Plugins import <code>@vectordevai/plugin</code>, including its
            <code>/tui</code> export. Local plugins and tools can keep a scoped SDK import when that module is unavailable;
            Vector supplies its own SDK without replacing a dependency that already resolves. New and published
            plugins should use the Vector imports explicitly. Theme manifests prefer <code>vector-themes</code>;
            keys ending in <code>-themes</code> remain accepted with the same path validation. The package will be published
            before the CLI release that installs it.
          </p>
          <p class="note">
            <code>--pure</code> on any command runs without external plugins. It is the first thing to try when you suspect one of breaking a run.
          </p>
</DocsPage>
